question_text:
You have a functional component that fetches user data on mount and displays it. The fetch should only run once. Which hook setup is correct?

```jsx
// Option A
useEffect(() => { fetchUser(); });

// Option B
useEffect(() => { fetchUser(); }, []);

// Option C
useEffect(() => { fetchUser(); }, [fetchUser]);

// Option D
useLayoutEffect(() => { fetchUser(); }, []);
```

A developer writes the following:

```jsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
setCount(count + 1);
setCount(count + 1);
setCount(count + 1);
```

After these three calls, what will count be?

What does the following component render on the first paint?

```jsx
function App() {
 const [show, setShow] = useState(true);
 useEffect(() => { setShow(false); }, []);
 return show ? <p>Hello</p> : null;
}
```

A component renders the number 0 from state when the list is empty:

```jsx
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
return <div>{items.length && <List items={items} />}</div>;
```

What does the user see when items is empty?

A developer has a component with this code:

```jsx
const onClick = useCallback(() => {
 console.log(count);
}, []);
```

The component renders a button that increments count. After clicking the button 5 times, they click the log button. What is printed?

Consider this component:

```jsx
function Timer() {
 const [time, setTime] = useState(0);
 useEffect(() => {
 const id = setInterval(() => setTime(time + 1), 1000);
 return () => clearInterval(id);
 }, []);
 return <p>{time}</p>;
}
```

What does the user see after 5 seconds?

You are building a custom useFetch hook. You want to ensure that if the component unmounts while a fetch is in-flight, the response is discarded and setState is not called. Which approach correctly implements this?

```js
// A
useEffect(() => {
 let cancelled = false;
 fetch(url).then(res => { if (!cancelled) setState(res); });
 return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, [url]);

// B
useEffect(() => {
 const controller = new AbortController();
 fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal }).then(setState);
 return () => controller.abort();
}, [url]);

// C
useEffect(() => {
 fetch(url).then(setState);
 return () => {};
}, [url]);

// D
useEffect(() => {
 const id = setTimeout(() => fetch(url).then(setState), 0);
 return () => clearTimeout(id);
}, [url]);
```

Consider this custom hook:

```js
function useValue(initial) {
 const [value, setValue] = useState(initial);
 const ref = useRef(value);
 ref.current = value;
 return [ref, setValue];
}
```

A developer uses ref.current inside an event handler. What problem does this solve and what new issue does it introduce?

In React 18 with automatic batching, which of the following setState calls will NOT be batched together?

```js
// A: Inside a React event handler
buttonEl.addEventListener('click', () => { setA(1); setB(2); });

// B: Inside a setTimeout
setTimeout(() => { setA(1); setB(2); }, 0);

// C: Inside an async function after an await
async function handler() { await fetchData(); setA(1); setB(2); }

// D: Inside a React synthetic event handler
<button onClick={() => { setA(1); setB(2); }}>
```

A component uses two contexts:

```jsx
const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
const user = useContext(UserContext);
```

ThemeContext updates frequently. The component only renders based on user, not theme. The developer adds React.memo. Do the re-renders stop?
